[36m(TaskRunner pid=255610)[0m We need to find the divisors \(d\) of \(9!\) such that \(d \equiv 1 \pmod{10}\). Since \(d \equiv 1 \pmod{10}\) implies \(d \equiv 1 \pmod{2}\) and \(d \equiv 1 \pmod{5}\), we can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem.
